+- [x] **Playwright coverage added for mobile viewport — a real device preset (iPhone 13's real
+      viewport, user-agent, and touch support, not just a resized window), and a real mobile
+      usability bug found and fixed, not just a test written around existing behavior.**
+      A real Playwright trace showed the hamburger menu click intercepted, over and over, by the
+      signed-in user-chip's own "Sign out" button — at a genuine 390px width, six icon buttons,
+      the user's name, "Sign out," and the hamburger were all crowded into one row with no
+      wrapping, meaning a real signed-in visitor on a real narrow phone could hit the exact same
+      problem: the hamburger becoming genuinely hard or impossible to tap. Fixed by hiding the
+      whole `.user-chip` below 480px (matching the same pattern already used for the desktop Admin
+      button) and adding "Sign out" to the mobile hamburger menu itself, so it's still reachable.
+      Also caught, while building this test: `devices["iPhone 13"]` defaults to the WebKit browser
+      engine (a real iPhone runs Safari, not Chromium) — this project only installs Chromium, so
+      the device's specific properties (viewport, user-agent, touch support) were pulled in
+      individually instead of spreading the whole preset, keeping this test on the same browser as
+      the rest of the suite. 4/4 tests confirmed passing for real.
+- [x] **Playwright coverage added for Cloudinary product photo upload — and it caught a real, live
+      production bug on its very first run, not a test-only issue.** The test uploads a real image
+      file to a real existing product, confirms the resulting stored URL is a genuine
+      `res.cloudinary.com` one (not a client-only base64 preview that never left the browser), then
+      restores the product's original photo afterward, since this deliberately touches real
+      catalog data, not test-only data it can freely discard.
+      **The real bug**: `CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME` on Railway was set to `"morning-aroma-app"` — the
+      *label* given to the Cloudinary API key, not the account's actual cloud name (Cloudinary
+      assigns its own identifier, e.g. `u4xbmlja`, unrelated to any name chosen at signup). Every
+      single photo upload attempt, by any admin, had been silently failing with "Invalid
+      cloud_name" — a real, live bug in production this whole time, only surfaced because writing
+      this test meant actually exercising the upload path for the first time, rather than trusting
+      that a feature marked "done" in an earlier round still genuinely worked. Confirmed both ways:
+      a manual upload in a real browser reproduced the exact same error before the fix, and
+      succeeded cleanly after correcting the Railway variable to the real cloud name.
+      **Real debugging, not guessed at**: the actual root cause was found by checking Railway's
+      logs directly for the failing request, not by iterating on the test in isolation — the same
+      "check real evidence first" discipline used throughout this whole file's development. A
+      genuine mistake was also caught and fixed along the way: an early attempt to harden
+      `registerCustomer` with the same retry pattern as `signIn` would have introduced a real bug
+      of its own — registration isn't idempotent the way logging in twice is, so blindly
+      resubmitting the same form on a retry could hit a genuine "account already exists" conflict
+      if the first attempt had actually succeeded server-side but was just slow to confirm on
+      screen. Fixed with a longer single timeout instead. A second real mistake, caught the same
+      way: raising the retry logic's per-attempt timeouts without raising the overall per-test
+      timeout to match meant Playwright's own default (30s) was silently killing tests mid-retry
+      before the new, longer retry logic ever got a fair chance — fixed by setting a real,
+      sufficiently long timeout for the whole file. 6/6 tests confirmed passing for real, against
+      the real, now-fixed production Cloudinary configuration.
